course: dictionary
lesson: 3169
title: Emphasis: the words that stand out
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Emphasis means special importance or attention given to something. When a writer or speaker gives emphasis to a word, that word matters more than the others. «The school puts emphasis on reading» tells you that reading is one of the things the school cares about most.
explain: When you speak, emphasis means saying a word or part of a word with extra force. «Put the emphasis on "not"» means you say "not" more strongly, so the listener knows it matters. Try saying «I did not take the biscuit» and stress a different word each time: the meaning changes every time.
explain: In writing, you can show emphasis with bold print, italics, underlining, capital letters or exclamation marks. Look at the clues around the word to see what the writer wants you to notice. Emphasis tells you where the main point is.
fact: Emphasis means special importance or attention given to something.
fact: In speech, emphasis means saying a word or syllable with extra force.
fact: In writing, bold, italics, underlining, capital letters and exclamation marks can show emphasis.
fact: «With emphasis» means said or written in a strong, clear way.
fact: In Singapore English, «lah» is added at the end of a sentence for friendly emphasis.
exercise: «The school puts emphasis on reading.» What does emphasis mean here? | A loud shout | *Special importance or attention | A short holiday | A mistake in spelling
exercise: «Put the emphasis on "not".» Where should the speaker put the extra force? | Quietly, on every word equally | On the last word of the sentence | On the first word of the sentence | *On the word not
exercise: Which of these is a way to show emphasis in writing? | Leaving out all the full stops | Writing in pencil | Making the page smaller | *Using bold print
test: Mum says, «The emphasis is on being kind.» What is the most important thing she wants you to do? | Being quiet | Being tall | Being late | *Being kind
test: In the sentence «I really want to go!», the word really is in italics. What does the italics show? | That the word is a name | That the word is spelt wrong | That the sentence is a question | *That the word should be said with emphasis
exam: The lesson's emphasis is on reading aloud with expression. Which words does the teacher say with extra force? | The words on the board | The first word of every line | The words that are shortest | *The key words that matter most
